<p>We also gratefully acknowledge the <strong>Smart India Hackathon (SIH) 2026</strong> organizing committee and the <strong>Ministry of Ayush</strong> for presenting a real-world, industry-relevant problem statement (SIH26044) that provided the foundational motivation for bridging the critical disconnect between higher educational curricula and modern industrial workforce expectations.</p>

<br clear=all style='mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always'>

<h1>ABSTRACT</h1>
<p>The rapid evolution of industrial technologies has widened the structural gap between university curricula and contemporary job role expectations. Graduating students often struggle to identify the exact technical competencies demanded by employers, while institutions lack automated visibility into curriculum deficiencies, and placement officers face substantial friction in assessing cohort-wide placement readiness.</p>
<p>To resolve these challenges, this project presents the <strong>Academia–Industry Skill Mapping and Placement Portal</strong>, an intelligent full-stack software system developed for <strong>Smart India Hackathon 2026 (Problem Statement ID: SIH26044)</strong>. The system is architected as a modular 4-tier platform comprising a Presentation Layer (React.js, Tailwind CSS, Vite), an Application Layer (Python FastAPI REST APIs), a Skill Intelligence Layer (contextual token-boundary Natural Language Processing, canonical synonym taxonomy normalization, and TF-IDF cosine semantic matching), and a Data Layer (relational PostgreSQL/SQLAlchemy database with 16 entity tables).</p>
<p>The platform introduces a strict operational separation between academic curriculum coverage (what is taught) and verified student competency (what an individual student can demonstrate through coursework, projects, and certifications). It calculates placement readiness using both unweighted and importance-weighted coverage formulas (wi in [1, 5], ci in {1.0, 0.5, 0.0}). For missing competencies, the engine generates prioritized, gap-targeted learning pathways linking students to verified educational resources across NPTEL, Coursera, edX, and FreeCodeCamp.</p>
<p>To evaluate system efficacy without fabricated claims, an empirical benchmark suite was executed on a manually validated ground-truth dataset comprising authentic university syllabi and industry job descriptions. The proposed contextual NLP pipeline achieved a <strong>Precision of 0.9630 (96.3%)</strong>, <strong>Recall of 0.8667 (86.7%)</strong>, and an <strong>F1 Score of 0.9123 (91.2%)</strong>, significantly outperforming a naive keyword-matching baseline which scored an F1 of <strong>0.7059 (70.6%)</strong>, representing a <strong>+20.6% absolute F1 performance gain</strong>. The platform also features role-based access control (RBAC) across five user roles, interactive shortage heatmaps, audit logging, and downloadable CSV/PDF placement readiness reports.</p>

<br clear=all style='mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always'>

<h1>CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION AND PROBLEM STATEMENT</h1>
<h2>1.1 Background and Problem Statement</h2>
<p>In modern engineering and higher education ecosystems, technological paradigms evolve rapidly. Advancements in cloud computing, microservices, artificial intelligence, electronic healthcare standards, and containerization have fundamentally transformed the competencies required by industry recruiters. However, traditional academic curricula undergo revision cycles that typically span several years. Consequently, a substantial disconnect emerges between what students are taught in lecture halls and what employers require in production environments.</p>
<p>As articulated in SIH 2026 (Problem Statement ID: SIH26044, Ministry of Ayush):<br>
<em>"Students cannot easily identify the skills required for their target job roles, while employers struggle to find candidates who possess those skills. Institutions require automated systems to map academic curricula to industry job requirements, identify skill gaps for student cohorts, and provide actionable placement-readiness insights."</em></p>

<h2>1.2 User Roles and Stakeholder Matrix</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Students:</strong> View personalized skill gaps, target roles, and ranked learning recommendations.</li>
  <li><strong>Faculty Members:</strong> Review curriculum mappings, validate extracted skills, and monitor syllabus alignment.</li>
  <li><strong>Placement Officers:</strong> Analyze cohort readiness, examine aggregate skill shortages, and export reports.</li>
  <li><strong>Industry Partners / Employers:</strong> Ingest job descriptions and configure skill importance weights ($w_i$).</li>
  <li><strong>Administrators:</strong> Oversee user roles, maintain canonical skill taxonomies, and inspect audit logs.</li>
</ul>

<br clear=all style='mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always'>

<h1>CHAPTER 6: SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND TECHNOLOGY STACK</h1>
<h2>6.1 4-Tier Architectural Design</h2>
<p>The application is structured into four distinct layers:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Presentation Layer:</strong> React 18 single-page application styled with Tailwind CSS and Lucide icons. Includes dedicated workspaces for Students, Faculty, Placement Officers, Employers, Skill Mapping, and Model Evaluation.</li>
  <li><strong>Application Layer:</strong> Python FastAPI REST API server organizing business logic into modular routers with Pydantic schema validation, JWT authentication, and RBAC guards.</li>
  <li><strong>Skill Intelligence Layer:</strong> Contextual regex pattern matcher with word boundaries, canonical synonym normalizer, Scikit-Learn TF-IDF vectorizer, and mathematical gap calculators.</li>
  <li><strong>Data Layer:</strong> 16 relational database tables mapped with SQLAlchemy ORM, supporting both SQLite and PostgreSQL.</li>
</ol>

